body: |-
  ## The A* Analogy

  A* search has three properties that make it the default pathfinding algorithm everywhere:
  1. **Admissible heuristic** — it never overestimates the remaining cost, so it converges to a good solution
  2. **Bounded cost** — you pay a known computational price, never infinite
  3. **Works with imperfect information** — the heuristic doesn't need to be perfect, just reasonable

  Paradigm maps to these exactly:
  1. **The symbol system is the heuristic** — # $ ^ ! ~ don't capture every nuance of a codebase, but they capture enough to navigate it. .purpose files don't document everything, but they document enough that an agent (human or AI) can orient in seconds.
  2. **The stop hooks are the bound** — they enforce a minimum bar. You can't finish a session without updating .purpose files, you can't add routes without portal.yaml. The cost of compliance is fixed and known.
  3. **Works with imperfect adoption** — a team doesn't need to understand every aspect anchor or lore arc on day one. Follow the structure, let the hooks enforce it, and the benefits compound over time. The heuristic improves as the team internalizes the vocabulary.

  ## Why "Arcane but Yields Results" Is the Right Design Point

  Every durable methodology has this property. Git is arcane. SQL is arcane. Regular expressions are arcane. They persist because they work reliably across contexts, not because they're intuitive on first contact.

  The alternative — "intuitive but fragile" — is what most AI-assisted development looks like today. No structure, no symbols, no enforcement. The AI figures it out each time from scratch. It works for small projects but collapses at scale because there's no heuristic to guide the search.

  Paradigm accepts the upfront cost of learning five symbols and a handful of conventions in exchange for bounded, reliable traversal of any codebase, by any agent, at any scale. That's the A* tradeoff.
